---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RemoveExifData from '@/components/tools/image-edit/RemoveExifData.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="remove-exif-data">
  <RemoveExifData client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to remove EXIF and other metadata</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop in the photos you want to share. The tool parses each one and lists every tag it found.</li>
      <li>Expand a file to read the full list: camera body, lens, serial number, exposure, timestamps, captions, and GPS coordinates if present.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Remove metadata</strong>. The tool decodes each image to raw pixels and encodes a new file from them, so no metadata can carry over.</li>
      <li>The tool parses the cleaned file again and shows the tag count. A green “verified clean” badge means the second parse found nothing.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What EXIF data a photo holds</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Block</th><th>Typical contents</th><th>Privacy risk</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>EXIF: camera</td><td>Make, model, body and lens serial numbers, firmware</td><td>Links each photo you post to the same physical camera</td></tr>
          <tr><td>EXIF: exposure</td><td>Shutter, aperture, ISO, focal length, flash</td><td>Low, but shows your gear and habits</td></tr>
          <tr><td>EXIF: timestamps</td><td>Original, digitised and modified dates, timezone offset</td><td>Puts you at a location at a specific minute</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GPS IFD</td><td>Latitude, longitude, altitude, heading, accuracy</td><td>High: the exact spot where the shutter fired, to a few metres</td></tr>
          <tr><td>IPTC</td><td>Caption, headline, byline, credit, city, country, keywords</td><td>Names, employers and locations added by editing software</td></tr>
          <tr><td>XMP</td><td>Editing history, software used, ratings, catalogue IDs</td><td>Shows your workflow and sometimes local file paths</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Maker note</td><td>Vendor-specific data, sometimes face-detection results</td><td>Undocumented and unpredictable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Thumbnail</td><td>A small JPEG preview embedded in the file</td><td>Can still show the uncropped original image</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Some editors update the main image but not the embedded thumbnail, so a cropped photo can still
      hold a small copy of what you cropped out. Re-encoding from pixels removes the thumbnail with
      everything else.
    </p>

    <h2>Why re-encoding is the reliable method</h2>
    <p>
      There are two ways to strip metadata. One is to walk the file structure and delete the segments the
      parser recognises. It is fast and keeps the compressed data exactly, but it removes only what the
      parser knows. Vendor extensions, unusual XMP packets and application-specific segments can get through.
    </p>
    <p>
      The other is to decode the image to a raw pixel buffer and write a new file from it. Only pixels go
      into the new file, so no other data can remain. This tool uses that method. The cost is one
      re-compression pass for JPEG output, and at quality 95 that generation loss is invisible. PNG output
      is pixel-perfect but larger.
    </p>

    <h2>Which channels strip metadata</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Metadata removed?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Facebook, Instagram, X, LinkedIn posts</td><td>Yes: images are re-encoded on upload</td></tr>
          <tr><td>WhatsApp / Signal / Telegram “photo” mode</td><td>Usually yes (the image is recompressed)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>WhatsApp / Telegram “document” or “file” mode</td><td>No: the original file is sent unchanged</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Email attachments</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cloud drive share links</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>AirDrop and Bluetooth transfer</td><td>No (unless you turn off location in the share sheet)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Marketplace and classified-ad listings</td><td>Varies; do not rely on it</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Your own website or CMS upload</td><td>Depends on the software</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>When you should keep the metadata</h2>
    <p>
      Metadata is a problem only when it goes further than you intended. Keep it in your own archive: dates
      and camera settings make a library searchable, GPS gives a map view, and IPTC credit fields are how
      photographers get paid. Keep full metadata on the master copy and strip it from the copy you publish.
      To read all the tags first, use the <a href="/image-metadata-viewer">metadata viewer</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Turning geotagging off at the source</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>iPhone: Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Location Services → Camera → Never. Or, when you share, tap Options at the top of the share sheet and turn off Location.</li>
      <li>Android: open the Camera app → Settings → turn off “Save location” or “Location tags”.</li>
      <li>Dedicated cameras: turn off the built-in GPS, and unpair phone-based location logging in the companion app.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These settings affect only new photos. Photos already in your library still hold their coordinates;
      use this tool to remove them.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
